// NOTE: AREA IS NOT A LAYOUT NUMBER. Table IV's 'Area (F^2)' is literally
// transistor count x 9 F^2 (2T1SOT = 9*2, 3T2SOT = 9*3). 9 F^2 for a W =
// 3F device leaves no room for source/drain contacts, spacing or the
// MTJ/HM stack footprint; a realistic 3F-wide MRAM access device is 12-16
// F^2. The 27 F^2 for 3T2SOT is therefore an optimistic lower bound.
// NOTE: The same table assigns 40.33 F^2 to the SINGLE 1T1MTJ STT access
// transistor - a 4.5x area ratio against the 9 F^2 SOT transistor for only
// a ~2x drive-current ratio (75 uA vs 33 uA). This asymmetric sizing
// assumption is what produces the paper's conclusion that SOT is denser
// than STT, and it is the opposite ordering from Liao 2020 and Seo & Kwon
// 2020. Treat it as an assumption, not a result.
// NOTE: The cell stores 1 bit in 2 MTJs and 3 transistors. If you model it
// in DESTINY as an ordinary 1-MTJ cell you will halve the storage-element
// count and get the wrong array energy. There is no DESTINY key for a
// differential 2-MTJ cell.
// NOTE: Write is UNIDIRECTIONAL and requires a read-before-write. Set and
// Reset currents/energies are identical by construction, which is unusual
// and worth a comment in the .cell file.
// NOTE: Temperature is never stated. 45 nm PTM default (typically 300 K /
// 27 C) is the reasonable assumption but the paper does not say so.
// NOTE: Abstract says 'the write latency of the proposed design can be
// reduced to 0.3 ns' while Table IV says 0.26 ns. Use 0.26 ns (the table).
// NOTE: TMR 90% is a literature value from W-SOT [ref 13], not measured on
// this stack; the measured Ta-SOT value in the same Table I is 55%.
// NOTE: completed by from_paper.py: CellAspectRatio borrowed from
// sot_hanjiang_aipadv2024_16nm
// NOTE: MLC synthesised, not measured. Every Soft* value is assumed. Sweep
// soft_ratio and report the range, or drop the MLC row.

